# Today's poem is PoetryDB's when the reader is online and a bundled one when
# it is not, so the route asserts what both share: the day's heading and a
# quote card made from the page on screen.
wait-for Make a quote card
# Today's poem is drawn again when PoetryDB answers, and a screen drawn
# again starts with its bar out of sight, so the touch waits for it.
wait-idle 3000
# The bar is out of sight on a poem until the top is touched.
tap-at 536,12
wait-idle
expect Today's poem
clean
shot verses-today
# A touch on the page puts the bar away again, and does nothing else.
tap-at 536,700
wait-idle
tap Make a quote card
wait-for Save a copy
expect Ready for your computer.
clean
shot verses-card

# The offline shelf, filed by poet, and a sonnet. At the default size the
# sonnet is whole on one page, and at the largest it pages without losing a
# line; unit tests hold both. The browse list takes more pages at the larger
# sizes, so the route turns to the sonnet.
tap Back
wait-for Make a quote card
wait-idle 3000
tap Browse
wait-for Poems
expect The Lamb
clean
shot browse
tap-paged How Do I Love Thee?
wait-for How do I love thee?
clean
shot sonnet
# Back returns to the page of the list the sonnet was chosen from.
tap Back
wait-for Browse
expect How Do I Love Thee?
